2026-10-13 — K01b: shared upstream QEMU MT-TCG disposition, classification only.

This record classifies the concurrent instruction-publication failure. It is
not the unchanged sched_ext replay, a cause of the original NULL fault, an
emulator repair, or K13. The installed emulator and the locked lanes are
unchanged; MT-TCG and HVF use the locked lanes, and the single-thread controls
use a diagnostic contract copy that only adds lanes.

Installed emulator: /opt/homebrew/bin/qemu-system-aarch64, QEMU 11.1.2,
SHA-256 2239f9f1d01b6c06764fada9300c5701990c3f113b1c3533a19c993f08096642.
Its Homebrew formula builds download.qemu.org/qemu-11.1.2.tar.xz with no
source patch. That tarball has SHA-256
731b5681e4bb18be313231579b8efd0296c5b015fa36dc533874b639ba838016 and a good
signature from release key CEACC9E15534EBABB82D3FA03353C9CEF108B584
(Michael Roth), fetched from keyserver.ubuntu.com into a scratch keyring.
The September 11.1.1 private builds and their captures were deleted with
.build/floating-targeted; their two patches remain in tests/fixtures.

Source path in QEMU 11.1.2 (system mode, a store to a page with code):
  accel/tcg/cputlb.c:2724 do_st4_mmu -> mmu_lookup -> 1691 mmu_watch_or_dirty
    -> 1337 notdirty_write -> 1345 tb_invalidate_phys_range_fast.
  accel/tcg/tb-maint.c:1210 takes the page lock at 1217, invalidates the
    overlapping blocks, and releases the lock at 1221.
  accel/tcg/cputlb.c:2733 do_st_4 -> 2665 store_atom_4 then writes the new
    word. This store comes after the unlock and holds no page lock.
  On another vCPU, accel/tcg/translate-all.c:261 tb_gen_code takes the
    page lock at 315 (it waits for that unlock), translates the OLD word at
    324, and links the block at 528. tb-maint.c:699 tb_page_add -> 715
    tlb_protect_code re-arms write detection. The pending store then lands.
    Nothing invalidates the new block until a later write to that page.
  Guest maintenance does not repair it. target/arm/helper.c:3534, 3539 and
    3544 define IC IALLUIS, IC IALLU and IC IVAU as ARM_CP_NOP in system
    mode; the 3471 ic_ivau_write invalidation is user-mode only.
    target/arm/tcg/translate-a64.c:2362 DSB emits a host barrier, and 2391
    ISB only ends the block, so the next lookup finds the stale block. The
    sync-core IPI and exception return also invalidate nothing.
  Commit deba78709ae8 (2023, "accel/tcg: Always lock pages before
  translation") closed the read-before-lock window. It does not cover this
  invalidate-then-store window.
  Single-thread TCG (accel/tcg/tcg-accel-ops-rr.c:181) runs one vCPU at a
  time and switches only between blocks, so a store helper finishes before
  another vCPU translates.
  HVF executes the guest's real cache maintenance.

The path is the same in the versions that produced earlier results:
tb-maint.c, translate-all.c, translator.c, helper.c and translate-a64.c are
byte-identical in v11.1.1 and 11.1.2. cputlb.c differs only in
probe_access_internal and the plugin lookup, and cpu-exec.c only in
cpu_exec_step_atomic. Both older patches,
qemu-instruction-publication.patch and qemu-cache-maintenance.patch, apply
to 11.1.2 with no offset or fuzz (patch --dry-run). The September results
of those private experiments therefore bind to the same source lines.

Upstream, checked 2026-10-13:
  master 615ece3c406b (2026-10-09): cputlb.c, tb-maint.c and
    translate-all.c are byte-identical to 11.1.2, and IC IVAU is still
    ARM_CP_NOP in system mode. No fix exists to test.
  QEMU issue #4669, opened 2026-10-10 and still open: "accel/tcg: MTTCG can
    run stale code after another vCPU's store to a code page". It names the
    same notdirty_write / tb_gen_code window for x86 ftrace text_poke_bp and
    shows memory holding the new byte while the stale block runs. Its one
    reply, from QEMU developer stsquad on 2026-10-13, asks whether the
    problem is x86-specific. No patch or merge request is linked. It cites
    the June 2025 LKML thread on x86 int3 oopses under QEMU 10.0 TCG, where
    the kernel side called it a QEMU bug.
  Mailing list: lore.kernel.org refused automated queries; a patchew subject
    search ("notdirty") and a web search found no 2026 qemu-devel patch for
    this window. The newest notdirty series is from 2023.
  This AArch64 evidence answers the x86-only question. The reduction
  replaces only an aligned direct B, an Arm-permitted concurrent
  modification, and runs DC CVAU, IC IVAU, DSB, ISB and a sync-core
  membarrier. The old branch still runs. Nothing was posted upstream from
  this project.

Reduced reproducer, unchanged instruction-publish-alias.c
(aea98940002005de23cd2071f61b0415d640a6d355b96c5a75d6534f9a3e576b).
Sealed evo-nexlab:sealed image e8d52bdced1fa49a5a56cde7e1173c54bd81832cc111441f6a31148ec03ead93,
network none: gcc-14 / clang-19 -static -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -pthread.
Both ELFs are byte-identical to the September 26 build:
  GCC 2ae439ae6ffca08b62ce4595ad67b53f9364ac52b9b3ca47e197295f5e015d1a
  Clang f230b17d75c4365c7705cd681870a5e311928fced746c895aa5daf358218996c
Six modes per compiler: concurrent B on one mapping, on RW/RX aliases, and
on aliases with a reader ISB; atomic memory gate; MOV under the write lock;
and a handshake run. 512 rounds, four CPUs, the source's 20-second alarm.
The 7,008-unit Images are retired (see K01). This uses the current pair:
  GCC virtconfig reference b6f206dea584b7e28f0c0ab255a9d6e68b30970f7e31f81210571c9929373f35
  EvoCC K16/K17 direct     add1c4874e28d2f1342d2ffa96fcbef8a7f2476416a761f356eda92c4034537f

                                     GCC kernel          EvoCC kernel
  MT-TCG, locked tcg-cortex-a72      6 PASS / 6 FAIL     6 PASS / 6 FAIL
  HVF, locked hvf-host               12 PASS / 0 FAIL    12 PASS / 0 FAIL
  ST-TCG, fixed instruction clock    12 PASS / 0 FAIL    12 PASS / 0 FAIL
  ST-TCG, host clock                 INVALID (alarm)     INVALID (alarm)

On MT-TCG the six failures per kernel are exactly the three concurrent-B
modes for each compiler. Misses (GCC kernel / EvoCC kernel):
  GCC one mapping            1751872 / 592192
  GCC aliases                1244800 / 412736
  GCC aliases plus ISB       1399104 / 314496
  Clang one mapping          1083136 / 672064
  Clang aliases               742400 / 610624
  Clang aliases plus ISB     1833216 / 737152
Every first bad snapshot returned 1 while memory held 14000001, the branch
to the return-zero pair. Memory-gate, locked-MOV and handshake controls have
zero misses on both kernels. On HVF and the fixed-clock ST control each
concurrent-B mode runs 8 to 50 million enabled calls with no miss.

The host-clock ST control is not evidence either way. Every program on both
kernels was killed by its own 20-second alarm (exit 142) before its summary;
load averages were 41 to 55 and the guest logged a 290 ms hrtimer interrupt.
The September 26 run of this control passed 12/12 per kernel. The fixed
clock lane (-accel tcg,thread=single -icount shift=0,sleep=off) keeps the
same single-thread scheduling, but guest time follows executed instructions,
so the alarm no longer measures host load. No program, alarm or assertion
changed. Load before each flight (1-minute average): MT 61.6/61.0,
HVF 48.3/47.0, host-clock ST 41.1/46.4, fixed-clock ST 55.4/60.6.
A host-clock ST retry started at load 23.4 (63.2 for the second kernel)
completed 2 of 24 programs, both PASS with zero misses; the other 22 hit the
alarm again (smc-retry/, results
1203ddccb6bcbd8ff07f1494fe394044dee26c13df4caccf2f87dfac7661ca7f).
Counts above are miss counts, not timing results.

Conclusion: the discrepancy is a shared upstream QEMU MT-TCG defect in
system-mode code publication, present in the installed 11.1.2 and current
master, affecting the GCC and EvoCC kernels alike. It is not an EvoCC
code-generation defect.

What remains:
  - The unchanged sched_ext hotplug -> reload_loop sequence and the complete
    23-test program are not replayed: their Images and payloads are retired
    (K01). A stale static-key branch under this defect is a plausible route
    to the old NULL fault; it is not proven.
  - There is no upstream fix. The private IC IVAU candidate covers only code
    that runs that maintenance; it is not a complete QEMU repair and was not
    rebuilt for 11.1.2.
  - The host-clock ST control needs a quiet host to be repeated; under this
    load its wall-clock alarm, not the code, decides the outcome.

